---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FitnessCaseStudy from '../../components/FitnessCaseStudy.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';
import { withBase } from '../../utils/paths';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return projects.map((project) => ({ params: { slug: project.slug }, props: { project } }));
}

const { project } = Astro.props;
const nextProject = projects[(projects.findIndex((item) => item.slug === project.slug) + 1) % projects.length];
const previewTitle = project.slug === 'ruchy-qr-business-card'
  ? 'Business-card preview'
  : project.slug === 'smart-qr-business-card'
    ? 'Business-card service preview'
    : project.slug === 'treatsbyrich'
      ? 'Project image'
      : project.category === 'Analytics'
        ? 'Dashboard or notebook preview'
        : 'Website preview';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${project.slug === 'supplier-quality-performance' ? 'Supplier Quality & Performance Dashboard' : project.title} | Issa Kareem Oyaifo`}
  description={project.summary}
  canonicalPath={`/work/${project.slug}/`}
>
  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="case-hero">
      <div class="case-hero__inner">
        <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href={withBase('/#work')}>Selected work</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>{project.category}</span></nav>
        <p class="eyebrow">{project.slug === 'saas-financial-analysis' ? 'FP20 Analytics Challenge 25' : project.type}</p>
        <h1>{project.slug === 'supplier-quality-performance' ? 'Supplier Quality & Performance Dashboard' : project.title}</h1>
        <p class="case-hero__summary">{project.slug === 'cafe-sales-analysis' ? 'Real-World Analytics Engagement | Python | Jupyter Notebook | Power BI' : project.slug === 'x24-music-intelligence' ? 'Independent Research Project | Power BI | Music Analytics | Data Research' : project.slug === 'hr-analytics' ? 'ZoomCharts HR Analytics Challenge | Power BI | DAX | ZoomCharts' : project.slug === 'pharmacy-sales-profitability' ? 'Power BI | DAX | ZoomCharts | Sales & Financial Analysis' : project.slug === 'credit-risk-analysis' ? 'Onyx Data Challenge | Power BI | Power Query | DAX | ZoomCharts' : project.slug === 'stock-exchange-analysis' ? 'Practical Financial Data Analysis | Python | Jupyter Notebook | yfinance' : project.slug === 'clinic-analysis' ? 'EDNA Scholarship Learning Project | Python | Jupyter Notebook | Power BI' : project.slug === 'fitness-centre-analytics' ? 'Onyx Data Challenge | Power BI | Power Query | Python | Jupyter Notebook | DAX | ZoomCharts' : project.slug === 'supplier-quality-performance' ? 'Power BI | Power Query | DAX | Data Modelling' : project.slug === 'saas-financial-analysis' ? 'Power BI | DAX | ZoomCharts' : project.summary}</p>
        <div class="case-hero__meta"><span>{project.category}</span>{project.tools.length > 0 && <span>{project.tools.join(' / ')}</span>}</div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <div class="container case-body">
      <article class="case-content">
        {project.slug === 'stock-exchange-analysis' ? (
          <>
            <nav class="case-article__toc" aria-label="Case study sections">
              <span class="case-article__toc-label">On this page</span>
              <div class="case-article__toc-items">
                <a href="#stock-overview">Project Overview</a>
                <a href="#stock-context">Project Context &amp; Objectives</a>
                <a href="#stock-tools">Tools &amp; Technologies</a>
                <a href="#stock-acquisition">Data Sources &amp; Acquisition</a>
                <a href="#stock-preparation">Data Inspection &amp; Preparation</a>
                <a href="#stock-workflow">Technical Workflow &amp; Code</a>
                <a href="#stock-methodology">Moving-Average Methodology</a>
                <a href="#stock-visual-analysis">Visual Analysis &amp; Interpretation</a>
                <a href="#stock-takeaways">Key Takeaways</a>
                <a href="#stock-limitations">Limitations &amp; Considerations</a>
                <a href="#stock-skills">Skills Demonstrated</a>
              </div>
            </nav>

            <section id="stock-overview">
              <h2>1. Project Overview</h2>
              <p>Historical market data helps analysts describe how an asset's price has changed, compare movement over different time horizons, and communicate the shape of a time series. This practical Python project uses Apple's historical share prices as its primary series and rolling averages as descriptive references for short-, medium- and longer-window price behaviour.</p>
              <p>The Jupyter Notebook requests market data, inspects the downloaded structure, prepares a single Apple price series, calculates three rolling means and visualises the result. It explores historical movements; it does not establish why prices moved, evaluate investment returns, predict future prices or recommend a trade.</p>
              <figure class="case-image">
                <img
                  class:list={project.imageFit === 'contain' ? 'case-image__dashboard' : undefined}
                  src={withBase(project.image ?? '/images/projects/stock-exchange-analysis.webp')}
                  alt={project.imageAlt ?? ''}
                  width={project.imageWidth ?? 950}
                  height={project.imageHeight ?? 550}
                  fetchpriority="high"
                />
                <figcaption>{project.imageCaption}</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <p class="stock-notebook-link"><a class="text-link" href={withBase('/downloads/Stock%20Exchange.ipynb')} download>Download the Jupyter Notebook <span aria-hidden="true">&#8595;</span></a></p>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-context">
              <h2>2. Project Context &amp; Objectives</h2>
              <p>Historical share prices are time-series data whose short-, medium- and longer-window movements can be difficult to compare in a single view. A moving average smooths a rolling set of past observations: shorter windows follow recent changes more closely, while longer windows provide a smoother and more lagged historical reference.</p>
              <p>The objective is to examine Apple's historical price series alongside 20-, 50- and 200-observation rolling averages. This is exploratory historical analysis, not a study of price causes, investment performance, strategy profitability or future prices.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-tools">
              <h2>3. Tools &amp; Technologies</h2>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Python and Jupyter Notebook:</strong> The environment used to run and document the analysis.</li>
                <li><strong>yfinance:</strong> Requested historical market data.</li>
                <li><strong>Pandas:</strong> Used to inspect tabular data, select the Apple price field, handle missing observations and calculate rolling means.</li>
                <li><strong>Matplotlib:</strong> Created a static historical-price chart and a static chart with moving averages.</li>
                <li><strong>Plotly Express:</strong> Created an interactive historical-price line chart.</li>
                <li><strong>Plotly Graph Objects:</strong> Built the final interactive chart from individual price and moving-average traces.</li>
                <li><strong>NumPy and Seaborn:</strong> NumPy is checked for package availability, and Seaborn is imported in the chart cell. The notebook does not demonstrate analytical use of either.</li>
                <li><strong>scikit-learn and statsmodels:</strong> Checked during environment setup only; neither is used to build a model in this notebook.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-acquisition">
              <h2>4. Data Sources &amp; Acquisition</h2>
              <p>The notebook uses yfinance to request daily historical data for Apple plus six additional market instruments. The additional tickers provide other stocks and indices in the downloaded result; the subsequent preparation and moving-average analysis focus on Apple.</p>
              <div class="stock-ticker-grid">
                <p><code>AAPL</code> — Apple</p>
                <p><code>GOOGL</code> — Alphabet</p>
                <p><code>MSFT</code> — Microsoft</p>
                <p><code>^GSPC</code> — S&amp;P 500</p>
                <p><code>TSLA</code> — Tesla</p>
                <p><code>^FTSE</code> — FTSE 100</p>
                <p><code>^N225</code> — Nikkei 225</p>
              </div>
              <p>Start and end dates are calculated at execution time. The end date is the current date; the start date is calculated as five times 365 days earlier. `group_by='ticker'` requests ticker-grouped columns, and `threads=False` disables threaded downloads. The notebook does not explicitly set interval, `auto_adjust`, retry policy or fixed dates.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`tickers = ["AAPL", "GOOGL", "MSFT", "^GSPC", "TSLA", "^FTSE", "^N225"]

end_date = datetime.today().strftime('%Y-%m-%d')
start_date = (datetime.today() - timedelta(days=5*365)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d')

data = yf.download(
    tickers,
    start=start_date,
    end=end_date,
    group_by='ticker',
    threads=False
)`}</code></pre>
              <p class="stock-evidence-note"><strong>Saved-output caveat:</strong> The notebook's download cell records a Yahoo Finance rate-limit error and seven failed downloads. Later saved cells nevertheless contain populated Apple data, including a 1,299-row sample with 45 missing values before cleaning. These saved outputs do not establish a clean, consistent end-to-end rerun. Treat the displayed counts as saved notebook output, not as guaranteed results of a future execution.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-preparation">
              <h2>5. Data Inspection &amp; Preparation</h2>
              <p>Data inspection uses `.head()` to preview Apple, Tesla and the wider downloaded data; `.columns` to list Apple's available fields; `len()` to count the Apple rows in the saved output; and `.isnull().sum()` to count missing values by field. The recorded Apple preview reports 1,299 rows and 45 missing values in the shown fields.</p>
              <p>For analysis, the notebook prefers Apple's `Adj Close` field when present and falls back to `Close`. It copies the selected field, renames it to `Adjusted_Close`, forward-fills missing observations with the last known value, then drops any remaining rows containing nulls. In the saved preparation output, the fallback message indicates `Close` was selected; the missing-value count shown after cleaning is zero.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`if 'Adj Close' in data['AAPL'].columns:
    aapl = data['AAPL'][['Adj Close']].copy()
    aapl.rename(columns={'Adj Close': 'Adjusted_Close'}, inplace=True)
else:
    aapl = data['AAPL'][['Close']].copy()
    aapl.rename(columns={'Close': 'Adjusted_Close'}, inplace=True)
    print("⚠️ Using Close price because 'Adj Close' not found.")

aapl.fillna(method='ffill', inplace=True)
aapl.dropna(inplace=True)`}</code></pre>
              <p>The saved output also contains a Pandas `FutureWarning` for `fillna(method='ffill')`, noting that this argument form is deprecated in that runtime. The notebook's code is shown as implemented rather than silently updated.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-workflow">
              <h2>6. Technical Workflow &amp; Code Examples</h2>
              <h3>Stage 1 – Environment setup</h3>
              <p>The notebook checks package availability with `importlib` for yfinance, Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, Seaborn, Plotly, scikit-learn and statsmodels. A second check collects missing imports, invokes pip through the active notebook interpreter using `sys.executable -m pip install`, and retries the imports. The saved output says yfinance was missing, the installation command completed, and the packages were then importable. Checking scikit-learn and statsmodels is not evidence that they were used for modelling.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`packages = ["yfinance", "pandas", "numpy", "matplotlib",
            "seaborn", "plotly", "sklearn", "statsmodels"]

cmd = [sys.executable, "-m", "pip", "install"] + missing
subprocess.check_call(cmd)`}</code></pre>
              <h3>Stage 2 – Financial data acquisition</h3>
              <p>Using the seven-ticker list and dynamically calculated dates, `yf.download()` requests the historical data with ticker grouping and threaded downloads disabled. The Apple series is selected for the later analysis.</p>
              <h3>Stage 3 – Data inspection</h3>
              <p>The notebook previews data with `.head()`, checks Apple fields with `.columns`, counts its rows with `len()`, and checks missing observations using `.isnull().sum()`. These steps show the available structure and missing-value counts before preparation.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`print(data["AAPL"].columns)
print(len(data["AAPL"]))
print(data["AAPL"].isnull().sum())
data["AAPL"].head()`}</code></pre>
              <h3>Stage 4 – Apple data preparation</h3>
              <p>The conditional selection, column rename, forward fill and removal of remaining nulls are shown in section 5. The source field is determined by the columns returned by yfinance.</p>
              <h3>Stage 5 – Historical price exploration</h3>
              <p>Matplotlib renders a blue static line against the date index. The source code labels its vertical axis "Sock Price (USD)". Plotly Express creates a second interactive line chart, changes its line to red, and labels the axes Date and Price (USD). These are separate static and interactive views of the prepared Apple series.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`plt.plot(aapl.index, aapl['Adjusted_Close'],
         color='blue', linewidth=2)
plt.title("Apple (AAPL) Stock Price Trend - Last 5 Years")
plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("Sock Price (USD)")
plt.show()

fig = px.line(
    aapl, x=aapl.index, y='Adjusted_Close',
    title="Apple (AAPL) Stock Price Trend - Interactive View",
    labels={"Adjusted_Close": "Price (USD)", "Date": "Date"}
)
fig.update_traces(line_color='red')
fig.show()`}</code></pre>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-methodology">
              <h2>7. Moving-Average Methodology</h2>
              <p>The notebook calculates rolling arithmetic means from the prepared `Adjusted_Close` series:</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`aapl['MA20'] = aapl['Adjusted_Close'].rolling(window=20).mean()
aapl['MA50'] = aapl['Adjusted_Close'].rolling(window=50).mean()
aapl['MA200'] = aapl['Adjusted_Close'].rolling(window=200).mean()`}</code></pre>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>20-day:</strong> averages 20 observations in each rolling window. It responds more quickly to recent price changes and makes shorter-window movement easier to compare.</li>
                <li><strong>50-day:</strong> averages 50 observations, giving a smoother and slower-moving reference than the 20-day line.</li>
                <li><strong>200-day:</strong> averages 200 observations, giving a still smoother, longer-window historical reference that responds more slowly.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>These are descriptive calculations over historical observations. They are lagging references and do not guarantee future direction or provide a validated trading signal.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-visual-analysis">
              <h2>8. Visual Analysis &amp; Interpretation</h2>
              <p>In the saved final chart, the actual price line varies more sharply than the rolling averages. The 20-day average follows changes more closely than the 50-day and 200-day averages because its window includes fewer observations. The longer-window averages appear smoother and lag the more immediate movement.</p>
              <p>The final Plotly Graph Objects figure adds four line traces: actual `Adjusted_Close`, `MA20`, `MA50` and `MA200`. Its saved layout sets a dark Plotly theme, Date x-axis, Price (USD) y-axis, and unified x-position hover. The notebook specifies a 950-by-550 figure. Its interactive view lets an analyst inspect aligned values across the series; the existing chart image above is the saved visual asset, not a newly generated chart.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`fig = go.Figure()

fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(
    x=aapl.index,
    y=aapl['Adjusted_Close'],
    mode='lines',
    name='Actual Price',
    line=dict(color='blue', width=2)
))

fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(
    x=aapl.index,
    y=aapl['MA20'],
    mode='lines',
    name='20-Day MA',
    line=dict(color='orange', width=2)
))

fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(
    x=aapl.index,
    y=aapl['MA50'],
    mode='lines',
    name='50-Day MA',
    line=dict(color='green', width=2)
))

fig.add_trace(go.Scatter(
    x=aapl.index,
    y=aapl['MA200'],
    mode='lines',
    name='200-Day MA',
    line=dict(color='red', width=2)
))`}</code></pre>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`fig.update_layout(
    title='Apple (AAPL) Stock with 20, 50, and 200-Day Moving Averages',
    xaxis_title='Date',
    yaxis_title='Price (USD)',
    template='plotly_dark',
    hovermode='x unified',
    width=950,
    height=550
)`}</code></pre>
              <p>The notebook does not report particular crossover dates, percentage gains, investment returns, trading signals or forecast values. The work focuses on visual trend exploration rather than quantified investment performance.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-takeaways">
              <h2>9. Key Takeaways</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>The notebook implements environment checks, multi-ticker data retrieval, Apple-field selection, missing-value handling, rolling calculations and chart construction.</li>
                <li>The final chart brings historical Apple prices and 20-, 50- and 200-observation rolling averages into one interactive view.</li>
                <li>The shorter window responds more closely to recent observations; longer windows provide smoother, more lagged historical context.</li>
                <li>The saved download cell reports rate limiting while later cells contain populated Apple data, so a clean end-to-end retrieval is not verified by the saved notebook state.</li>
              </ul>
              <h3>Potential business relevance</h3>
              <p>This kind of visualisation can support historical market-trend monitoring, exploratory technical research, comparison of shorter- and longer-window historical direction, and communication of financial time-series data. These are potential applications; the notebook itself performs data retrieval and visual exploration and does not carry out investment decisions, trading, backtesting or formal forecasting.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-limitations">
              <h2>10. Limitations &amp; Considerations</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>The notebook does not demonstrate a backtested trading strategy or calculate buy/sell signals.</li>
                <li>It does not establish predictive accuracy and does not provide a formal investment recommendation.</li>
                <li>The five-year start date is recalculated from the execution date, so the retrieved window changes over time.</li>
                <li>The selected price field depends on the fields returned by yfinance: `Adj Close` is preferred when present; otherwise the notebook uses `Close`.</li>
                <li>Moving averages are calculated from past observations. They are descriptive and lagging references, not guaranteed predictors.</li>
                <li>The saved download output reports Yahoo Finance rate limiting and failed downloads, while later cells retain populated Apple data. The saved notebook does not verify a clean end-to-end rerun.</li>
                <li>The saved output shows that `fillna(method='ffill')` raised a Pandas deprecation warning in that environment.</li>
                <li>No transaction-cost model, portfolio construction, return/risk evaluation, or formal comparison of the additional tickers is demonstrated.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="stock-skills">
              <h2>11. Skills Demonstrated</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Financial market data retrieval with the yfinance library.</li>
                <li>Data inspection and preparation using Pandas.</li>
                <li>Missing-observation checks, forward filling and removal of remaining null rows.</li>
                <li>Rolling-window calculations for 20-, 50- and 200-day means.</li>
                <li>Historical time-series exploration.</li>
                <li>Static chart creation with Matplotlib.</li>
                <li>Interactive chart development with Plotly Express and Plotly Graph Objects.</li>
                <li>Communicating what a chart shows while distinguishing historical description from prediction or investment advice.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
          </>
        ) : project.slug === 'fitness-centre-analytics' ? (
          <FitnessCaseStudy project={project} />
        ) : project.slug === 'clinic-analysis' ? (
          <>
            <nav class="case-article__toc" aria-label="Case study sections">
              <span class="case-article__toc-label">On this page</span>
              <div class="case-article__toc-items">
                <a href="#clinic-overview">Project Overview</a>
                <a href="#clinic-context">Background &amp; Objectives</a>
                <a href="#clinic-tools">Tools &amp; Technologies</a>
                <a href="#clinic-data">Dataset Overview &amp; Source</a>
                <a href="#clinic-cleaning">Python Data Cleaning</a>
                <a href="#clinic-features">Feature Engineering</a>
                <a href="#clinic-eda">Exploratory Analysis</a>
                <a href="#clinic-power-query">Power Query &amp; Python</a>
                <a href="#clinic-dashboard">Power BI Dashboard</a>
                <a href="#clinic-questions">Analytical Questions</a>
                <a href="#clinic-limitations">Limitations</a>
                <a href="#clinic-skills">Skills &amp; Learning</a>
              </div>
            </nav>

            <section id="clinic-overview">
              <h2>1. Project Overview</h2>
              <p>This self-directed project was developed while learning Python through the EDNA Scholarship programme. It follows clinic records from CSV inspection and preparation in a Jupyter Notebook through exploratory analysis and reporting in Power BI. The analysis focuses on recorded financial fields and the timing of patient entries; it is a learning project, not a client engagement or production healthcare system.</p>
              <div class="clinic-kpi-grid" aria-label="Selected displayed figures">
                <div class="clinic-kpi"><strong>29,999</strong><span>Rows reported by the notebook's initial data inspection</span></div>
                <div class="clinic-kpi"><strong>6,915</strong><span>Count of Patient ID in the available Corporate-filtered screenshot</span></div>
                <div class="clinic-kpi"><strong>$256K</strong><span>Total Revenue card in that same displayed screenshot</span></div>
              </div>
              <figure class="case-image case-image--gallery clinic-dashboard-image">
                <img
                  class="case-image__dashboard"
                  src={withBase(project.image ?? '/images/projects/clinic-analysis.png')}
                  alt={project.imageAlt ?? ''}
                  width={project.imageWidth ?? 1280}
                  height={project.imageHeight ?? 724}
                  fetchpriority="high"
                />
                <figcaption>{project.imageCaption}</figcaption>
              </figure>
            </section>

            <section id="clinic-context">
              <h2>2. Project Background &amp; Objectives</h2>
              <p>Clinic datasets can place financial entries, patient categories and event timestamps in the same record. Preparing these fields consistently makes it possible to examine how recorded activity varies across categories and time. Here, the notebook derives financial and hour/day fields and uses descriptive charts to explore those relationships.</p>
              <p>The project objective was to inspect and prepare the data in Python, investigate weekday and hour patterns in patient entries and the notebook's calculated financial total, then carry prepared information into Power BI. The analysis does not measure waiting time, doctor productivity, profitability or operational savings.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="clinic-tools">
              <h2>3. Tools &amp; Technologies</h2>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Python and Jupyter Notebook:</strong> The environment used to load the CSV, document transformations and explore results.</li>
                <li><strong>Pandas:</strong> Used for CSV loading, column preparation, financial conversion, missing-value handling, feature creation, aggregation, pivot tables and CSV export.</li>
                <li><strong>Matplotlib:</strong> Used for figures in the notebook and for setting a dark plotting style.</li>
                <li><strong>Seaborn:</strong> Used to create boxplots, violin plots, heatmaps and two-dimensional KDE density plots.</li>
                <li><strong>Power Query:</strong> Used to import the original and prepared CSV files, assign data types and make a Python transformation within a query.</li>
                <li><strong>Power BI:</strong> Used to present two report pages, Financial Class and Doctor Type, with category views and patient/revenue visuals.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="clinic-data">
              <h2>4. Dataset Overview &amp; Source</h2>
              <p>The notebook loads <code>hospital_data.csv</code> into a Pandas DataFrame and its saved <code>data.info()</code> output reports 29,999 records and 12 source columns. The fields include Date, Medication Revenue, Lab Cost, Consultation Revenue, Doctor Type, Financial Class, Patient Type, Entry Time, Post-Consultation Time, Completion Time, Doctor and Patient ID. The initial output shows the fields as object/string data, including the formatted financial values.</p>
              <p>The notebook is the evidence for the Python workflow and its saved outputs. The Power BI file contains report pages titled Financial Class and Doctor Type. The Power Query source scripts supplied with the project describe separate imports of the original CSV and the processed export.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="clinic-cleaning">
              <h2>5. Python Data Cleaning Process</h2>
              <p>The notebook first replaces the original spaced column labels with a clean 12-name list, then applies <code>str.strip()</code>. A custom function handles the three financial text columns: it preserves nulls for later treatment, converts values to strings, removes currency symbols, underscores, hyphens, commas and spaces, then uses <code>pd.to_numeric(..., errors='coerce')</code>.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`def clean_text(x):
    if pd.isnull(x):
        return x
    x = str(x)
    x = x.replace('$', '')
    x = x.replace('_', '')
    x = x.replace('-', '')
    x = x.replace(',', '')
    x = x.replace(' ', '')
    x = pd.to_numeric(x, errors='coerce')
    return x

data['Medication Revenue'] = data['Medication Revenue'].apply(clean_text)
data['Lab Cost'] = data['Lab Cost'].apply(clean_text)
data['Consultation Revenue'] = data['Consultation Revenue'].apply(clean_text)`}</code></pre>
              <p>After those conversions, the notebook runs <code>data = data.fillna(0)</code> across the whole DataFrame. That changes missing values to zero in financial and non-financial columns alike. It assumes every missing value can be treated as zero, which may not be appropriate, and the notebook does not demonstrate validation against the source system.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="clinic-features">
              <h2>6. Feature Engineering &amp; Time Extraction</h2>
              <h3>Notebook financial total</h3>
              <p>The field named <code>Total Revenue</code> is calculated row by row by adding Medication Revenue, Lab Cost and Consultation Revenue:</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`data['Total Revenue'] = (
    data['Medication Revenue']
    + data['Lab Cost']
    + data['Consultation Revenue']
)`}</code></pre>
              <p>This is the notebook's sum of the three named fields, including Lab Cost. It is not evidence of profit or net income, because the calculation does not establish that all operating expenses have been deducted; the field name should not be read as a validated accounting measure.</p>
              <h3>Hour and weekday features</h3>
              <p>A helper converts each time value with <code>pd.to_datetime()</code> and returns its hour. The notebook applies it to Entry Time, Post-Consultation Time and Completion Time. These are hour-of-day features; the notebook does not subtract the timestamps to calculate waiting time or consultation duration.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`def get_hour(x):
    x = pd.to_datetime(x)
    x = x.hour
    return x

data['Entry Hour'] = data['Entry Time'].apply(get_hour)
data['Post Consultation Hour'] = (
    data['Post-Consultation Time'].apply(get_hour)
)
data['Completion Hour'] = data['Completion Time'].apply(get_hour)`}</code></pre>
              <p>The Date field is converted to datetime, then represented as both a numeric weekday index (Monday is 0 through Sunday as 6) and a weekday name. The numeric value supports plotting against the day axis; the name is used for grouping and readable heatmap columns.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`data['Date'] = pd.to_datetime(data['Date'])
data['dayofweek'] = data['Date'].dt.dayofweek
data['weekday name'] = data['Date'].dt.strftime('%A')`}</code></pre>
            </section>

            <section id="clinic-eda">
              <h2>7. Exploratory Data Analysis</h2>
              <p>The notebook sets Matplotlib's <code>dark_background</code> style and uses Pandas, Matplotlib and Seaborn for descriptive exploration. It groups <code>Total Revenue</code> by weekday and sums the values; the saved output ranges from $97,480.16 on Thursday to $266,648.00 on Monday. These amounts reflect the notebook's calculated field and should not be interpreted as profit or as independently validated clinic revenue.</p>
              <p>A Seaborn boxplot compares the distribution of row-level Total Revenue by weekday with outlier markers hidden (<code>showfliers=False</code>), while the violin plot shows the distribution shape by weekday. These are distribution views, not causal explanations.</p>
              <h3>Weekday and entry-hour pivot tables</h3>
              <p>For revenue activity, <code>pivot_table</code> sums Total Revenue for each Entry Hour and weekday. Seaborn renders the resulting matrix as a heatmap; the notebook repeats this revenue heatmap with different colour maps. A second pivot counts Patient ID by the same hour/day combinations for a patient-activity heatmap.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`pivot = pd.pivot_table(
    data,
    index='Entry Hour',
    columns='weekday name',
    values='Total Revenue',
    aggfunc='sum'
)`}</code></pre>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`pivot = pd.pivot_table(
    data,
    index='Entry Hour',
    columns='weekday name',
    values='Patient ID',
    aggfunc='count'
)`}</code></pre>
              <p>The two-dimensional KDE plot maps numeric weekday to Entry Hour and fills the density contours with the <code>magma</code> colour map. It is a smoothed density visual, not a count table. Together, the heatmaps and KDE make weekday/hour patterns easier to inspect; the notebook does not state verified peak counts or explain why any pattern occurs.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`sns.kdeplot(
    x=data['dayofweek'],
    y=data['Entry Hour'],
    fill=True,
    cmap='magma'
)`}</code></pre>
              <p>Finally, the notebook exports the processed DataFrame with <code>data.to_csv('cleaned_clinic_data.csv')</code>. The resulting file can be selected as a Power BI input; because <code>index=False</code> is not specified, Pandas also writes the DataFrame index.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="clinic-power-query">
              <h2>8. Power Query &amp; Python Integration</h2>
              <h3>Original CSV preparation</h3>
              <p>A supplied Power Query script imports the original CSV with <code>Csv.Document</code>, promotes headers and assigns field types. It replaces <code>$-</code> with <code>0</code> in Lab Cost and converts that column to Currency.Type. This is a separate Power Query preparation route on the original input; it is distinct from the notebook's Python cleaning and its exported cleaned file.</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`Source = Csv.Document(
    File.Contents("...hospital_data.csv"),
    [Delimiter=",", Columns=12, Encoding=1252,
     QuoteStyle=QuoteStyle.None]
)`}</code></pre>
              <h3>Notebook-prepared CSV import</h3>
              <p>The supplied cleaned-data query imports 19 columns, assigns types to the date, financial, category, time, person-ID and derived hour/weekday fields, and renames the first blank column to <code>Index</code>. This describes its column name only; the project evidence does not establish a further business meaning for that field.</p>
              <h3>Python transformation inside Power Query</h3>
              <p>Separate from the Jupyter Notebook workflow, the supplied Power Query Python script creates a weekday-by-entry-hour count pivot from its <code>dataset</code> input, renames the resulting columns to weekday abbreviations and resets the index:</p>
              <pre class="case-article__code"><code>{`import pandas as pd

pivot = pd.pivot_table(
    dataset,
    index='Entry Hour',
    columns='weekday name',
    values='Patient ID',
    aggfunc='count'
)

pivot.columns = ['Mon', 'Tue', 'Weds', 'Thurs', 'Fri', 'Sat', 'Sun']
pivot = pivot.reset_index()`}</code></pre>
              <p>This query transformation prepares a patient-count matrix for the report. It does not replace or extend the notebook calculations unless the report query specifically uses that output.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="clinic-dashboard">
              <h2>9. Power BI Dashboard Walkthrough</h2>
              <p>The PBIX contains two report pages, Financial Class and Doctor Type. The available screenshot below is the Financial Class view with Corporate selected. It shows category navigation for Corporate, HMO, Insurance, Medicare and Private; KPI cards for Count of Patient ID and Total Revenue; a patient-entry trend combining patient count with revenue; a revenue activity heatmap; and a patient activity density visual.</p>
              <p>The Power BI file also contains a Doctor Type page with Anchor, Floating and Locum categories and the same described patient/revenue, trend and time-activity visual structure. A separate Doctor Type screenshot was not available among the project image files, so no replacement image has been created.</p>
